# 8. Your IPv6 Verification Output

You then ran:

```text
cafe01-RT01#show ipv6 int brief
```

The important section is:

```text
GigabitEthernet0/0.10      [up/up]
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
2001:DB8:1:1::1

GigabitEthernet0/0.20      [up/up]
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
2001:DB8:1:2::1
```

This is the confirmation that your IPv6 configuration worked.

---

# 9. What `[up/up]` Means

For example:

```text
GigabitEthernet0/0.10 [up/up]
```

means:

```text
Interface status = up
Line protocol    = up
```

So the subinterface is operational.

Same for:

```text
GigabitEthernet0/0.20 [up/up]
```

Both VLAN subinterfaces are operational.

---

# 10. The `FE80::` Address

This is one of the most important observations in the lab.

You manually configured:

```text
2001:DB8:1:1::1
```

but the router also shows:

```text
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
```

You didn't manually configure that address.

That's because IPv6 interfaces automatically have/use a **link-local address**.

The important prefix is:

```text
FE80::/10
```

So:

```text
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
```

is a **link-local IPv6 address**.

---

# 11. You Have Two IPv6 Addresses on the Interface

This is a major IPv6 concept.

For `GigabitEthernet0/0.10`:

```text
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
2001:DB8:1:1::1
```

These serve different purposes.

### Link-local

```text
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
```

Used for communication on the **local IPv6 link**.

### Global unicast

```text
2001:DB8:1:1::1
```

The configured IPv6 address for the VLAN's IPv6 subnet.

So conceptually:

```text
Gi0/0.10
     │
     ├── FE80::...              ← Link-local
     │
     └── 2001:db8:1:1::1/64    ← Configured IPv6 address
```

---

# 12. Why Is the Same `FE80::` Showing on Both Subinterfaces?

Your output shows:

```text
Gi0/0.10
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866

Gi0/0.20
FE80::201:42FF:FEC9:7866
```

This is something worth noticing.

In this Packet Tracer/Cisco lab environment, the same link-local address is being displayed on both subinterfaces.

The key point for **this lesson** is not to conclude that every Cisco implementation must always generate the same link-local address on every interface. The important observation is:

> **The `FE80::` address appeared automatically even though you only configured the global IPv6 address.**

The next lesson goes deeper into why link-local addresses exist and how they're used.

---

# 13. Notice the Difference Between the IPv4 and IPv6 Configuration

Your router now effectively has:

### VLAN 10

```text
IPv4:
10.0.18.1

IPv6:
2001:db8:1:1::1/64
```

### VLAN 20

```text
IPv4:
10.0.18.33

IPv6:
2001:db8:1:2::1/64
```

So you haven't removed the IPv4 addresses.

You've added IPv6.

That's the **overlay/coexistence concept**.

```text
VLAN 10
├── IPv4 → 10.0.18.1
└── IPv6 → 2001:db8:1:1::1

VLAN 20
├── IPv4 → 10.0.18.33
└── IPv6 → 2001:db8:1:2::1
```

